---
/**
 * ConversationStackFailure.astro — V4
 *
 * Visualizes the v1 numbered-list failure mode specific to the agetech /
 * Carestar case: the GPT held the full thread in memory, the user only
 * saw what was on screen, and a single-character reply ("1") got matched
 * against the wrong list. The fix wasn't a better list — it was an
 * interaction model that didn't depend on lists in scrolling chat at all.
 *
 * Side-by-side framing makes the architectural problem visible: same
 * input, two contexts, different (wrong) outcome. Mobile collapses to
 * a vertical stack with the annotation underneath.
 */
---

<figure class="v4-csf">
  <header class="v4-csf-head">
    <span class="v4-csf-eyebrow">Conversational stack failure · v1, cohort 1</span>
    <p class="v4-csf-tagline">The GPT held the whole thread in memory. The user could only see what was on screen.</p>
  </header>

  <div class="v4-csf-grid">
    <article class="v4-csf-pane v4-csf-memory">
      <header class="v4-csf-pane-head">
        <span class="v4-csf-pane-label">What the GPT had in memory</span>
        <span class="v4-csf-pane-meta">First numbered list · earlier in thread</span>
      </header>
      <div class="v4-csf-thread">
        <div class="v4-csf-msg v4-csf-msg-ai">
          <div class="v4-csf-role">Coach</div>
          <div class="v4-csf-bubble">
            Quick screening — for each, just answer yes or no:<br />
            <span class="v4-csf-list">
              1. Diabetes (Type 2)<br />
              2. Heart failure or coronary artery disease<br />
              3. COPD or another chronic lung condition<br />
              4. Cancer, current or in the past five years<br />
              5. Stroke or significant cognitive decline<br />
              <span class="v4-csf-ellipsis">… continues to 10</span>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="v4-csf-msg v4-csf-msg-user">
          <div class="v4-csf-role">Caregiver</div>
          <div class="v4-csf-bubble">No, no, yes, no, no, yes, no, no, no, no.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="v4-csf-pane-foot">
        <span class="v4-csf-foot-mono">… many turns of probes, follow-ups, and reflection in between …</span>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="v4-csf-pane v4-csf-screen">
      <header class="v4-csf-pane-head">
        <span class="v4-csf-pane-label">What the user could see on screen</span>
        <span class="v4-csf-pane-meta">Second numbered list · current view</span>
      </header>
      <div class="v4-csf-thread">
        <div class="v4-csf-msg v4-csf-msg-ai">
          <div class="v4-csf-role">Coach</div>
          <div class="v4-csf-bubble">
            A few more for the daily-living picture:<br />
            <span class="v4-csf-list">
              1. Has Robert had any falls in the past six months?<br />
              2. Any new hospitalizations?<br />
              3. Any new medications added recently?
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="v4-csf-msg v4-csf-msg-user">
          <div class="v4-csf-role">Caregiver</div>
          <div class="v4-csf-bubble v4-csf-bubble-flag">1</div>
        </div>
        <div class="v4-csf-msg v4-csf-msg-ai">
          <div class="v4-csf-role">Coach</div>
          <div class="v4-csf-bubble v4-csf-bubble-error">
            Got it — confirming <strong>diabetes</strong>. A few follow-ups about Robert's blood sugar management…
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="v4-csf-pane-foot">
        <span class="v4-csf-foot-mono">User answered the falls question. The GPT matched against the first list.</span>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <aside class="v4-csf-annot">
    <span class="v4-csf-annot-label">What this taught us</span>
    <p>This wasn't a user error. The GPT had the full conversation in memory; the user only had what was on screen. Stack sequential numbered lists in a scrolling chat window and you'll lose context the model doesn't know is gone. The v2 fix wasn't a better list — it was an interaction model where lists in chat were no longer the primary input shape.</p>
  </aside>
</figure>

<style>
  .v4-csf {
    margin: 3rem 0;
  }
  .v4-csf-head {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }
  .v4-csf-eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--font-mono, 'DM Mono', monospace);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--gold-text, #8A6820);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .v4-csf-tagline {
    font-family: var(--font-display, 'Petrona', Georgia, serif);
